This version treats every candidate as two choices. It can be picked, in which case the index stays the same, or skipped, in which case the index moves forward. Keeping the same index on the pick branch is what allows values to appear more than once.

The stored list must be copied. The same temporary list is changed later while the recursion goes back.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    private void build(int[] candidates, int index, int remaining,
                       List<Integer> current, List<List<Integer>> answer) {
        if (remaining == 0) {
            answer.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
            return;
        }

        if (remaining < 0 || index == candidates.length) {
            return;
        }

        current.add(candidates[index]);
        build(candidates, index, remaining - candidates[index], current, answer);
        current.remove(current.size() - 1);

        build(candidates, index + 1, remaining, current, answer);
    }

    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum(int[] candidates, int target) {
        List<List<Integer>> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        build(candidates, 0, target, new ArrayList<>(), answer);
        return answer;
    }
}
```

A target of `0` returns one empty combination, while a target smaller than every candidate returns an empty list. Candidates are assumed to be positive, as required by the problem.

Time is exponential in the worst case. The recursion stack and current list use `O(target / minCandidate)` space, excluding the output.

This uses a small breadth-first style expansion. Each queue item stores a partial combination, its sum, and the next candidate index. From one item, later candidates are added one at a time. Reusing the same candidate is done by putting the same index into the new item.

Because the next index never moves backward, `[2, 3]` is created but `[3, 2]` is not. That keeps the result free of order-based duplicates.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    private class State
    {
        public List<int> Values;
        public int Sum;
        public int Start;

        public State(List<int> values, int sum, int start)
        {
            Values = values;
            Sum = sum;
            Start = start;
        }
    }

    public List<List<int>> CombinationSum(int[] candidates, int target)
    {
        var answer = new List<List<int>>();
        var queue = new Queue<State>();
        queue.Enqueue(new State(new List<int>(), 0, 0));

        while (queue.Count > 0)
        {
            State state = queue.Dequeue();

            if (state.Sum == target)
            {
                answer.Add(new List<int>(state.Values));
                continue;
            }

            for (int i = state.Start; i < candidates.Length; i++)
            {
                int nextSum = state.Sum + candidates[i];
                if (nextSum > target)
                {
                    continue;
                }

                var nextValues = new List<int>(state.Values);
                nextValues.Add(candidates[i]);
                queue.Enqueue(new State(nextValues, nextSum, i));
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

For `[2, 3, 5]` and target `8`, the queue first holds one-number paths, then expands them into longer paths. It eventually records `[2, 2, 2, 2]`, `[2, 3, 3]`, and `[3, 5]`.

If the target is smaller than all candidates, the queue removes the empty state and returns no combinations. This is easy to follow, but copying lists into queue items can use a lot of memory.

Time is exponential in the number of generated partial combinations. Space includes the queue and copied paths, which can also be exponential. The input array is not changed.

Sorting makes the stopping rule simple. I keep a `start` index so a combination is built in one order only. When a candidate is chosen, the recursive call uses the same index because that number can be reused.

For `[2, 3, 6, 7]` and target `7`, one path is `2, 2, 3`. After reaching `7`, a copy of the current path is saved. The path is then shortened so other choices can be tested.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
private:
    void search(const vector<int>& candidates, int remaining, int start,
                vector<int>& current, vector<vector<int>>& answer) {
        if (remaining == 0) {
            answer.push_back(current);
            return;
        }

        for (int i = start; i < static_cast<int>(candidates.size()); ++i) {
            if (candidates[i] > remaining) {
                break;
            }

            current.push_back(candidates[i]);
            search(candidates, remaining - candidates[i], i, current, answer);
            current.pop_back();
        }
    }

public:
    vector<vector<int>> combinationSum(vector<int>& candidates, int target) {
        sort(candidates.begin(), candidates.end());

        vector<vector<int>> answer;
        vector<int> current;
        search(candidates, target, 0, current, answer);
        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity is exponential because the number of valid combinations can be large. If `m` is the smallest candidate, the recursion depth is at most `target / m`. The input array is sorted in place, and the extra working space is `O(target / m)`, not counting the returned combinations.

The recursive search below still uses pick and skip choices, but it memoizes the results for each pair of `(index, remaining)`. If the same state is reached again, its combinations are reused instead of being built from scratch.

Each memoized result is stored as complete suffix combinations. When the current candidate is picked, it is added to every combination returned by the state with the reduced remaining value.

```cpp
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
private:
    unordered_map<long long, vector<vector<int>>> memo;
    vector<int>* values;

    vector<vector<int>> solve(int index, int remaining) {
        if (remaining == 0) {
            return {vector<int>()};
        }
        if (remaining < 0 || index == static_cast<int>(values->size())) {
            return {};
        }

        long long key = (static_cast<long long>(index) << 32) | remaining;
        auto found = memo.find(key);
        if (found != memo.end()) {
            return found->second;
        }

        vector<vector<int>> answer;

        vector<vector<int>> picked = solve(index, remaining - (*values)[index]);
        for (vector<int> combination : picked) {
            combination.insert(combination.begin(), (*values)[index]);
            answer.push_back(combination);
        }

        vector<vector<int>> skipped = solve(index + 1, remaining);
        answer.insert(answer.end(), skipped.begin(), skipped.end());

        memo[key] = answer;
        return answer;
    }

public:
    vector<vector<int>> combinationSum(vector<int>& candidates, int target) {
        memo.clear();
        values = &candidates;
        return solve(0, target);
    }
};
```

The key is packed from the index and remaining target, so each state has one cache entry. For an input where no candidate can make the target, the recursion eventually reaches the end and returns an empty result.

The number of states is roughly `n * target`, but each state may copy many combinations. Therefore the real time and space cost also depends on the output size. The recursion depth is `O(n + target / minCandidate)`.

